.TH GIT-EZ 1 "November 23, 2026" "git-ez" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-ez \- local rebase and merge with AI-assisted conflict resolution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git ez rebase
.I target
.RI [ --onto " newbase"]
.RI [ --repo " directory"]
.RI [ --check " executable arguments..."]
.br
.B git ez merge
.I target
.RI [ --ff " | " --no-ff " | " --ff-only ]
.RI [ --repo " directory"]
.RI [ --check " executable arguments..."]
.br
.B git ez probe
.br
.B git ez audit list
.br
.B git ez audit show
.RI [ run-id ]
.RI [ --path " run-directory"]
.RB [ --verbose ]
.br
.B git ez --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Integrate a revision into the current branch using Git, calling a configured
LLM
when conflicts require resolution. Preserve an audit of changes, decisions,
and reasons. No branches or worktrees are created; no fetch or push is
performed.
.PP
A checked-out branch, clean checkout and no active Git operation are required.
Commit or stash local changes with native Git before running git-ez. Autostash
is unsupported: both operations pass --no-autostash, overriding rebase.autoStash
and merge.autoStash without changing the saved configuration.
The target is required and pinned at startup. Only simple rebase and merge are
supported; arbitrary Git options are not forwarded. Interactive rebase,
rebasing merge commits, merge --squash, --no-commit, octopus
merge,
and unrelated histories are unsupported. The tool cannot attach to an
operation
already started with Git.
.PP
Only one git-ez integration can run per checkout. A concurrent invocation fails
immediately, before repository checks, audit creation, or model calls. The lock
covers review, validation, and final audit capture. Linked worktrees have
independent locks. Native Git and other tools do not honor this advisory lock.
.PP
The lock is released when the process exits, even after abrupt termination.
The empty ez-execution.lock file remains in the checkout's Git directory;
its presence does not indicate an active run. Do not delete it during a run.
Git operations and local changes are preserved for inspection and native
recovery.
.PP
Rebase uses the merge backend with update-refs and rebase-merges disabled.
Autostash is explicitly disabled for both operations. Merge uses --no-edit and
respects Git's fast-forward policy. Git
runs
noninteractively with diff3 conflict markers. Hooks and signing are respected.
Caller configuration is otherwise retained, except repository-redirection
environment variables.
.PP
Valid model configuration is required even when no conflicts occur; no model
call is made when Git resolves the operation without assistance.
.SH PROBE
The probe checks a text response, a native call to the harmless
compatibility_echo tool, and subsequent use of its result through the configured
model connector. It does not expose repository contents or shell tools to the
model. A compatible result does not measure conflict-resolution quality.
.PP
The command writes a single final JSON report to standard output and diagnostics
to standard error. No intermediate snapshots are saved. Use shell redirection
to save the report:
.PP
.nf
git ez probe > report.json
.fi
.PP
A failed compatibility check is included in the JSON report. A configuration
error or interruption before completion may leave no report. The shell may
create or truncate the destination file even if the command fails.
.SH INSTALLATION ON FREEBSD
FreeBSD support is experimental. CI targets FreeBSD 15.1 amd64/Python 3.12
only.
ARM64 is outside the CI scope.
Install git, uv, python312, py312-sqlite3, rust, and pkgconf from FreeBSD
packages.
Use uv tool install with --python /usr/local/bin/python3.12 and the repository
URL or local checkout. The environment remains isolated from system Python
packages. Dependencies without FreeBSD wheels may compile from source.
Graph checkpoints stay in memory; no SQLite checkpoint adapter or sqlite-vec
is required. Events and offloaded agent files are retained in the audit
directory.
Internal graph states and pending tasks are not persisted for inspection or
restart.
Abrupt termination may leave incomplete audit files and skip final journal updates.
See docs/freebsd.md in the project repository for installation commands.
Development requirements and FreeBSD-specific tool warnings are documented in
CONTRIBUTING.md. Ruff is not required at runtime.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --keep-base, --no-keep-base
Rebase only. Keep the unique common ancestor with the upstream as the result's
base, or reset this policy. Implies --reapply-cherry-picks unless explicitly
disabled. Incompatible with --onto. Missing or ambiguous merge bases are
rejected.
The upstream tip need not be an ancestor of a successful result in this mode.
.TP
.B --fork-point, --no-fork-point
Rebase only. Use the named upstream reference's local reflog to refine replay
selection, or disable this policy. The last explicit CLI value wins. With the
required explicit upstream, fork-point defaults to disabled, including with
--keep-base or rebase.forkPoint=true. Like native Git, that configuration does
not enable fork-point for an explicit upstream. Use --fork-point to opt in.
Requires a named branch, remote-tracking branch, tag or HEAD. Use
--no-fork-point
for a literal SHA or revision expression. An unavailable reflog point falls
back
to the otherwise chosen replay boundary, with an explicit audit reason.
.PP
The engine pins the base and fork point before integration and passes explicit
--onto and --no-fork-point arguments. Divergent exclusion histories may require
an internal merge commit object to preserve native cherry-pick equivalence.
No branch or reference is created for this object and it is excluded from the
result. It is unsigned metadata; signing policy still applies to result
commits.
Native pre-rebase hooks receive this effective pinned
replay
boundary rather than the original upstream name. Hook failures are not
bypassed.
Hooks requiring the original upstream argument should use native Git directly.
.PP
When --onto equals the fork point and combining exclusions changes the replay
boundary, the native interactive sequencer avoids an incorrect up-to-date
shortcut.
A capture-only editor records its instructions without changing them.
Autosquash
remains disabled unless explicitly requested; the default empty policy remains
drop and per-commit reuse is preserved. The instruction-format override and
bounded sequence capture described below also apply to this case.
.PP
Original input references remain monitored. Final ancestry uses the
computed result base. These options combine with
autosquash
and signing; --root and rebasing merge commits remain unsupported.
.TP
.B --autosquash, --no-autosquash
Rebase only. Opt in to native grouping of fixup!, squash!, and amend! commits,
or disable it. The last explicit value wins. rebase.autoSquash alone does not
activate this mode. Git chooses the order and combined messages, with no editor
or model intervention except for resolving conflicts.
.PP
git-ez uses interactive machinery internally with a capture-only sequence
editor,
forces rebase.instructionFormat=%s for this invocation, and preserves the
default
--empty=drop. Explicit empty policies apply to replayed commits; Git handles
empty
fixup/squash resolutions within their existing groups. Interactive editing
remains
unsupported. Hooks and signing are retained.
.PP
The audit captures rebase-todo.txt before replay. A failed or over-limit
capture
stops Git before replay; missing evidence prevents overall success after
completion.
The capture editor records rebase-todo.txt.sha256 before replay. Changes to the
sequence or fingerprint, including from Git hooks, make the run fail.
Inspect final commits and messages with git log. Local state and available
evidence are preserved on failure.
.TP
.B --rerere-autoupdate, --no-rerere-autoupdate
Override rerere.autoupdate for the whole run, including continuation, without
changing stored configuration. Does not enable rerere.enabled. Resolutions
reused
and staged by native rerere are continued without a model call for
that conflict. With --no-rerere-autoupdate, reused file contents still pass
through
assisted resolution and validation while the index remains unmerged. The audit
records requested and effective autoupdate.
.TP
.B -m message, --message message
Merge only. Supply the merge commit message. Repeat for additional paragraphs.
Mutually exclusive with --file. Does not force a commit on fast-forward.
.TP
.B -F file, --file file
Merge only. Read a UTF-8 message file before integration. Paths are relative to
the invocation directory, including with --repo. Standard input (-F -) is
unsupported.
The message is recorded in the audit and retained after conflicts.
.TP
.B --log[=n], --no-log
Merge only. Include up to n incoming commit subjects in the message. Bare --log
uses 20; attach another nonnegative limit with --log=5. --no-log means --log=0.
The last explicit value wins. If omitted, merge.log applies. Git generates the
summary, including with -m or -F; it survives conflict continuation.
.TP
.B --cleanup=strip|whitespace|verbatim|scissors|default
Merge only. Apply Git's native cleanup mode to the message, also after
conflicts.
The prepared MERGE_MSG is preserved during continuation. No editor is opened;
scissors retains native noninteractive behavior. Omission uses native defaults
and configuration. Neither cleanup nor log forces a commit on fast-forward.
Requested policies are recorded in the audit.
.TP
.B -S[key], --gpg-sign[=key], --no-gpg-sign
Sign newly created commits or explicitly disable signing. Attach an optional
key
to the option; without one, Git's configured key is used. The policy remains
effective after conflicts and when retaining empty resolutions. Signing
failures
are not bypassed. Existing commits reused by Git are not signed again.
.TP
.B --signoff, --no-signoff
Add or omit the committer's Signed-off-by trailer on newly created commits.
This is distinct from a cryptographic signature. Hooks remain active.
.TP
.B --verify-signatures, --no-verify-signatures
Merge only. Verify the incoming tip commit with native Git trust settings, or
explicitly disable this verification. This does not verify every incoming
commit
or an annotated tag's signature. Explicit options override Git configuration.
.TP
.B --empty=drop|keep|stop
Rebase only. Control commits that become empty. Explicit policies also apply
after assisted conflict resolution: keep creates an empty commit with the
original
author and message, drop lets Git discard it, and stop leaves the active rebase
for manual handling with a nonzero exit code. This extends native continuation,
which can drop an empty resolution even with --empty=keep. Hooks and signing
remain active. Policies and empty resolutions are recorded in the audit.
.TP
.B --keep-empty, --no-keep-empty
Rebase only. Keep or discard commits that were already empty before replay.
.TP
.B --reapply-cherry-picks, --no-reapply-cherry-picks
Rebase only. Control whether Git replays changes equivalent to upstream
commits.
Reapplied commits that become empty are handled by --empty.
.TP
.B --force-rebase, -f, --no-ff, --ff
For rebase, force replay of unchanged commits; --ff resets this request. The
last
request wins. For merge, --ff and --no-ff instead select the policy described
below.
.TP
.B --onto newbase
Rebase only. Replay the commits selected after target onto newbase. Both
revisions
are pinned and their named references are checked throughout the run. The audit
records the upstream boundary and new base separately. Only the
current branch is rebased. A second positional branch argument and the A...B
new-base shorthand are unsupported.
.TP
.B --ff, --no-ff, --ff-only
Merge only. Allow fast-forward with a merge commit otherwise (--ff), create a
merge commit when integration is needed (--no-ff), or refuse divergent
histories
(--ff-only). An already up-to-date branch stays unchanged. An explicit policy
overrides Git configuration; the last policy specified wins. Without one, Git
configuration and defaults apply. A refused --ff-only merge does not call the
model or change the checkout. The requested policy is recorded in the audit and
report. Specify these options before --check.
.TP
.B --repo directory
Use this repository instead of the current directory. Subdirectories are
accepted.
.TP
.B --check executable arguments...
Run final validation without a shell at the repository root. Must be the last
option. The agent may also run this command during resolution.
.TP
.B -h
Display command-line usage. Each subcommand also accepts this option.
.TP
.B --help
Display this manual through Git. Requires the manual directory on MANPATH.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Use native Git configuration keys under ez.*, with normal Git scopes and
includes.
Set ez.model to the exact model identifier. The default provider is openai,
using a Chat Completions-compatible endpoint; ollama is also supported.
.PP
Set ez.endpoint for a custom server, ez.apiKeyEnv for a token environment
variable,
and ez.caBundle for an internal PEM CA bundle. Prefer environment variables to
ez.apiKey, which Git stores in plaintext. HTTPS is required outside loopback
unless ez.allowInsecureHttp is explicitly enabled.
.TP
.B ez.provider
openai or ollama; default: openai
.TP
.B ez.model
Required model identifier
.TP
.B ez.endpoint
Server URL; defaults to the OpenAI API or local Ollama, depending on the
provider
.TP
.B ez.caBundle
Optional trusted PEM CA bundle
.TP
.B ez.apiKeyEnv
Token environment variable; default: OPENAI_API_KEY or LOCAL_MODEL_API_KEY
.TP
.B ez.apiKey
Optional direct token, redacted from the audit
.TP
.B ez.allowInsecureHttp
Explicitly allow non-loopback HTTP; default: false
.TP
.B ez.timeout
HTTP, agent command, and validation timeout; default: 120 seconds
.TP
.B ez.minConfidence
Confidence threshold; default: 0.8
.TP
.B ez.maxFileBytes
Maximum proposal size and per-file review capture; default: 128 KiB
.TP
.B ez.maxRounds
Maximum number of conflict stops; default: 100
.TP
.B ez.modelContextTokens
Server context window; default: 32768
.TP
.B ez.modelMaxOutputTokens
Output token reserve; default: 8192
.TP
.B ez.agentMaxTurns
Model call budget per session; default: 40
.TP
.B ez.agentMaxToolCalls
Tool call budget per session; default: 100
.TP
.B ez.agentMaxContextBytes
Additional context size limit; default: 1000000 bytes
.TP
.B ez.toolOutputBytes
Captured shell output limit; default: 32768 bytes
.TP
.B ez.requestOptions
JSON object containing server options
.PP
See docs/configuration.md and examples/ez.gitconfig in the project repository
for provider setup and examples. Use probe to check native tool-call
compatibility
without exposing a repository or shell tools.
.SH AUDIT AND FAILURE RECOVERY
Audits are stored under ez/runs/<id> in the repository's common Git directory.
Each run prints its identifier and inspection command. Use git ez audit show
with the identifier, or --path with a copied run directory.
.PP
On failure or interruption, local state is retained without automatic
rollback.
Inspect the report and git status; continue or abort an active operation using
native Git commands (git rebase --continue / --abort or git merge --continue /
--abort). If integration completed but final validation failed, there is no
active
operation to continue or abort. There is no agent session resume command.
.PP
The agent shell has host-process privileges and is not a system sandbox.
Content checks apply to conflicted files and supporting files changed by the
agent.
Regular files integrated by Git without conflict are exempt when their contents
and
permissions remain unchanged during resolution, including binary and oversized
resources. Audit file capture limits still apply.
.PP
New runs use journal schema 4. For every conflict handled by the LLM, the
journal retains the conflict diff, resolution, explanation and confidence.
Original inputs are in conflicts/NNNN/request.json; conflict.diff compares
ours and theirs. resolution.txt and resolution.diff retain the proposal.
run.json records whether it was applied. Git remains the source for commit
history and repository diffs; native command archives are not retained.
.PP
A session can resolve multiple files with the same explanation and confidence.
Confidence is a model estimate, not independent validation. If a resolver fails
before returning a decision, available edits are retained when possible without
inventing an explanation or score. Conflict inputs and proposals must be saved
before staging. A final journal publication failure returns a nonzero result
and warns that saved state may be older; Git is never rolled back.
.PP
Model/tool events, agent files and optional validation output provide supporting
diagnostics. Diagnostic export and Markdown failures are warnings; validation output loss
does not change the validation result. These files
are not a complete graph history or recovery checkpoint. Missing or unreadable
conflict captures are reported explicitly. File reads do not follow symlinks.
.PP
audit list shows saved runs newest first, including their checkouts. audit show
selects the latest run or a full/unique abbreviated identifier. --path selects a
copied run directory and is exclusive of an identifier. --verbose adds saved
model/tool events and command outputs. Reprompts retain separate proposals,
explanations, confidence and feedback; all conflict diffs use the original ours.
Acceptance is distinct from application. Commands requested by the model retain
execution directory, timing, exit code, streams and truncation when available.
Native engine commands and repository-wide history are not archived.
.PP
Consultation uses the Git pager in a terminal and direct stdout for pipes and
redirections. GIT_PAGER, core.pager, pager.ez and Git's --no-pager/--paginate
conventions apply. Quitting the pager is normal; no second pager is opened.
.PP
show reads run.json and saved captures without model calls or repository
recapture, ignoring report.md. Reading does not certify evidence or reconstruct
history. Only schema 4 is supported; inspect older journals directly.
.PP
Known configured credentials are filtered. Audit files may contain sensitive
source code and output; review before sharing. Unexpected execution exceptions
propagate after best-effort cleanup. Resource budgets remain in effect.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Published integration success or a compatible probe. For show: a readable
journal, including a recorded failed or unfinished run.
.TP
.B 1
Failed admitted run, noninteractive interruption, failed publication, or failed
probe.
.TP
.B 2
Configuration, command-line, pre-admission, or delivery error. For show: no
readable journal is available. Cached Markdown is not a fallback.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted interactive integration run (SIGINT).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
git config --global ez.model exact-model-id
git config --global ez.endpoint https://ai-server.example/v1
git ez probe > /tmp/ai-compatibility.json
git ez rebase main
git ez merge feature
git ez rebase main --check python -B -m pytest
.fi
.SH INTERACTIVE PROPOSAL REVIEW
.B --interactive
is supported for merge and rebase. One global approval covers all files in the
current conflicting Git step. The complete diff and captured additions appear
automatically. Choose accept, reject/reprompt with feedback, or quit. This is
not
Git's interactive rebase sequence editor. Empty input never accepts; EOF stops
and Ctrl+C interrupts with exit status 130.
.PP
Each revision starts from the original tracked conflict baseline, with the
previous proposal and user feedback available to the harness. Final validation
runs after integration, as in noninteractive mode.
.PP
Quit and interruption restore the original tracked file content, modes and
unmerged
index, and preserve native merge/rebase metadata. They do not abort or reset
the operation. Resolve manually, git add the files,
and use native git rebase --continue or git commit. Each later conflict
requires
separate approval. Model commands and checks are terminated before
restoration.
Repeated SIGINT is ignored during restoration. A restoration failure is
reported
explicitly and the private temporary backup is retained for manual recovery.
If child termination cannot be confirmed, restoration is not attempted and the
backup is retained instead.
.PP
Interactive restoration covers paths tracked in the original conflict index.
Regular tracked contents already represented by their stage-zero Git blob are
restored from that blob; only other tracked contents, including exact conflict
markers, require a private temporary copy. The index and native operation
metadata are saved separately. No bundle, branch, stash or full worktree copy is
created. Unrelated nontracked directories are not traversed.
.PP
Nontracked and ignored files are neither backed up nor removed. Model/check
changes to these files, including newly created files, may persist after a
retry, Quit or SIGINT. Checks must manage their own outputs outside the tracked set.
If a nontracked file or directory blocks restoration of a tracked path, report
an error and retain the backup rather than discarding the nontracked data.
Tracked submodules and special filesystem files are unsupported. Avoid
concurrent edits.
Commands still run with host privileges: this is not an operating-system
sandbox.
SIGKILL, host crashes, escaped/daemonized child processes and external writes
are
outside this cleanup guarantee. There is no resumable git-ez session.
.PP
Engine confidence, file, reference and operation checks still apply. Changes
during review invalidate approval. Each proposal, feedback and decision is
retained in the audit separately from application. Noninteractive failures
retain
the existing best-effort behavior. No conflicts means no prompt or model call;
native rerere autoupdate resolutions do not pass through harness review.
